@startuml 15-sdd-failsafe-activity
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Diagram 15: Health Monitor Failsafe Decision Tree
' SDD §10.3 — health_monitor_node  (1 Hz decision loop)
' Redrawn 2026-09-08 against the real HEALTH-001..004 implementation
' (ws/src/health_monitor/) — see health_monitor_node.hpp's own top-of-file
' note for the FR-026-vs-SDD-§10.3 rationale this diagram now follows.
' MC-SDD-001
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

title "15 — Health Monitor Failsafe Decision Tree\nhealth_monitor_node  ·  1 Hz decision loop  ·  SDD §10.3"

skinparam BackgroundColor #FAFBFC
skinparam DefaultFontColor #1C2334
skinparam DefaultFontSize 10
skinparam TitleFontSize 13
skinparam TitleFontStyle bold
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam RoundCorner 5

skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #D8E8F8
  BorderColor #3D5A80
  BorderThickness 1.5
  FontSize 10
  DiamondBackgroundColor #FFE8C0
  DiamondBorderColor #C07000
  DiamondFontSize 9
  ArrowColor #3D5A80
  ArrowFontSize 9
  ArrowFontColor #2A4060
  StartColor #1A3860
  EndColor #C02020
}

skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFAE8
  BorderColor #C09030
  FontSize 9
}

start

:1 Hz WallTimer fires\n(sample_and_publish());

note right #FFE0E0
  <b>FR-026 vs SDD §10.3:</b> the SDD text (and FR-072/FR-075) reads as if
  health_monitor_node commands the FCC directly for HOLD/RTL. FR-026 is
  explicit and absolute instead: mission_manager_node is the sole node
  that commands a flight-mode change. Every branch below only publishes
  an annotated /mc/fault; mission_manager_node's own pre-existing
  on_fault()/is_critical_fault() machinery is what actually RTLs.
  <b>The one deliberate exception</b> is Branch B's FR-075 final-resort
  case: after 3 failed mission-exec.service restarts, mission_manager_node
  is presumed unreachable, so health_monitor_node commands RTL directly.
  That is the ONLY send_command() call anywhere in this node.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Also: all branches below run <b>sequentially</b>, in this exact order,
  within one single-threaded WallTimer callback — not concurrently. The
  fork/join grouping is only a readability device for six independent
  checks, not a claim of parallel execution.
end note

fork
  ' ─── Branch A: FCC link (check_fcc_link) ────────────────
  :Read /mc/fcc/vehicle_state.fcc_link_ok\n(published by fcc_bridge_node);
  if (fcc_link_ok?) then (no)
    if (was ok last tick?  (edge)) then (yes)
      :<color:#C02020>publish_fault(FCC_LINK_LOST, CRITICAL,\nACTION_RTL)</color>;
      note right
        Annotation only — no send_command() here.
        fcc_bridge_node already publishes its own
        FCC_LINK_LOST fault; mission_manager_node's
        existing on_fault()/is_critical_fault()
        (severity ≤ CRITICAL) is what actually RTLs.
        This is a second, independent observation
        for /mc/health visibility (defense-in-depth).
        [FR-026, FR-072]
      end note
    else (no — still down, already reported this outage)
    endif
  else (yes)
  endif
  :Set/clear FCC_LINK_LOST in fault_mask\nto match current fcc_link_ok  (every tick);

fork again
  ' ─── Branch B: Manager node liveness (check_manager_heartbeat) ──
  :node_watches_[mission_manager_node] alive?\n(heartbeat seen within\nmanager_heartbeat_timeout_s, default 5 s);
  if (alive now?) then (yes)
    :restart_count ← 0; escalated ← false;\nClear MANAGER_DEAD in fault_mask;
  else (no)
    :Set MANAGER_DEAD in fault_mask  (every tick);
    if (already escalated to RTL?) then (yes)
      :Do nothing further —\nedge-triggered, waits for recovery;
    else (no)
      if (just died this tick,\nOR ≥ manager_restart_retry_s (10 s)\nsince last action?) then (no — still cooling down)
        :Do nothing this tick;
      else (yes)
        :restart_count++;
        if (restart_count ≤ 3?) then (yes)
          :Request systemd restart via watchdog.sock\n(target: mission-exec.service);
          :<color:#E05000>publish_fault(MANAGER_DEAD, CRITICAL,\n"restart attempt N/3", ACTION_NONE)</color>;
        else (no — restart budget exhausted)
          :escalated ← true;
          :<color:#C02020>send_fcc_command(RTL)</color>\n<b>— the ONE branch that ever calls\nsend_command() (FR-075)</b>;
          :<color:#C02020>publish_fault(MANAGER_DEAD, CRITICAL,\n"restart budget exhausted — commanded\nRTL directly (FR-075)", ACTION_RTL)</color>;
        endif
      endif
    endif
  endif

fork again
  ' ─── Branch C: GCS link (check_gcs_link) ────────────────
  :Read /mc/gcs/link_status.gcs_link_ok;
  if (ok → lost this tick?  (edge)) then (yes)
    if (mission_phase in {INGRESS, MUSTER, ISR_SELECTION,\nVORONOI_PARTITION, SEARCH}\nAND override_gcs_link_hold_ not active?) then (yes)
      :publish_fault(GCS_LINK_LOST, WARNING, ACTION_HOLD);
      note right
        Severity WARNING (not CRITICAL) — deliberately
        does NOT trip mission_manager_node's blanket
        "any critical fault → RTL" reaction.
        <b>Known, documented gap:</b> mission_manager_node
        has no HOLD-on-fault path today, so nothing
        actually enacts HOLD yet — annotation/telemetry
        only, same class of accepted gap as
        OFFBOARD_STREAM_STALE / GIMBAL_LINK_LOST.
        [FR-073 intent, SFR-007 — tracked, not silently
        reinterpreted]
      end note
    else (no — safe phase, or override active)
      :publish_fault(GCS_LINK_LOST, WARNING, ACTION_NONE);
    endif
  else (no change, or already lost)
  endif
  :Set/clear GCS_LINK_LOST in fault_mask\nto match current gcs_link_ok;

fork again
  ' ─── Branch D: Battery (check_low_battery) ──────────────
  :Read battery_pct from /mc/fcc/vehicle_state\n(ignore -1.0 "unknown" sentinel\nuntil a real sample arrives — TEST-002);
  if (0 ≤ battery_pct < effective threshold?\n(default 20%, or mission_upload's\nper-mission override)  — edge only) then (yes)
    if (override_low_battery_rtl_ active?) then (yes)
      :publish_fault(LOW_BATTERY, WARNING,\n"suppressed by operator override", ACTION_NONE);
    else (no)
      :<color:#C02020>publish_fault(LOW_BATTERY, CRITICAL,\nACTION_RTL)</color>;
      note right
        Annotation only, same pattern as Branch A —
        no send_command() here either. CRITICAL
        severity is enough for mission_manager_node's
        existing is_critical_fault() to auto-RTL
        on its own. [FR-026]
      end note
    endif
  else (no)
  endif
  :Set/clear LOW_BATTERY in fault_mask;

fork again
  ' ─── Branch E: GPU thermal (check_gpu_overtemp) ─────────
  :Read gpu_temp_c  (tegrastats pipe,\nvia ResourceSampler);
  if (gpu_temp_c > gpu_overtemp_threshold_c\n(default 85 °C)?  — edge only) then (yes)
    :Set perception_down_node param\ninference_rate_limit_fps → 5 FPS\n(AsyncParametersClient, best-effort —\nnode may not exist yet);
    :publish_status_text(WARNING,\n"GPU temperature ... exceeds threshold");
    :publish_fault(GPU_OVERTEMP, WARNING, ACTION_NONE);
  else (no)
  endif
  :Set/clear GPU_OVERTEMP in fault_mask;

fork again
  ' ─── Branch F: vision.service cgroup memory (check_vision_memory) ──
  ' INFRA-007 — not in the original SDD §10.3 text; added after this
  ' diagram was last drawn. Time-windowed, not edge-on-first-sample.
  :Sample vision.service cgroup\nmemory_current / memory_max  %;
  if (usage ≥ vision_mem_threshold_pct\n(default 80%), sustained\n≥ vision_mem_sustained_s (default 30 s)?\n— edge only) then (yes)
    :publish_fault(VISION_MEM_HIGH, WARNING,\n"MemoryMax=6G budget at risk", ACTION_NONE);
  else (no)
  endif
  :Set/clear VISION_MEM_HIGH in fault_mask;

end fork

:Publish /mc/health\n(all metrics + fault_mask + node_alive_mask);
:Publish MC_HEALTH (50103) → GCS via gcs_mavlink_node;

note right
  There is no "CPU_OVERTEMP" fault or decision branch —
  mc_msgs/FaultEvent.msg defines no such constant, and
  no code path samples CPU temperature into a failsafe
  decision (only GPU_OVERTEMP and VISION_MEM_HIGH above).
  An earlier version of this diagram showed one; removed.
end note

end

@enduml
